        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A zero-turn mower is a speed tool. It earns its price on large, mostly level lawns full of trees and beds, where it can spin around obstacles and mow at a pace no lawn tractor matches. It is a poor fit for steep ground, for towing, and for buyers who choose deck width by acreage alone. Get the terrain question right first and the rest of the decision is about how long you want the machine to last.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />


        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The 6 Best Zero-Turn Mowers, Ranked
          </h2>

          <p>
            These rankings draw on published manufacturer specifications (transaxle model, deck construction, engine, warranty), dealer network reach, and consistent themes in owner feedback. We did not run these mowers through our own field trials; where a point rests on a spec sheet or owner reports, that is stated.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} index={index} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="is-it-right" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Is a Zero-Turn the Right Machine for Your Property?
          </h2>

          <p>
            A zero-turn mower steers by driving its two rear wheels at different speeds through a pair of hydrostatic transaxles. Push one lap bar forward and pull the other back and the mower spins in place. That design makes it far faster around trees, beds and fence lines than a lawn tractor, which has to make wide arcs and three-point turns. On a property with a lot of obstacles, a zero-turn can cut total mowing time dramatically.
          </p>

          <p>
            The format has limits that the speed pitch skips over. Zero-turns have no steering wheel and steer only through the rear wheels, so they lose control more easily on slopes and wet grass. Most are not designed to tow heavy loads or run ground-engaging attachments. If you need to haul a dump cart, pull an aerator across a sloped yard or plow snow, a lawn tractor or a <a href="/best-compact-tractor/">compact tractor</a> is the better tool.
          </p>

          <p>
            As a rough guide, a zero-turn makes sense once you have more than about an acre of mostly level grass to cut. Below that, a good lawn tractor or even a wide walk-behind is usually cheaper and simpler. Our <a href="/best-riding-lawn-mower/">riding mower guide</a> covers both lawn tractors and small zero-turns if you are on the border.
          </p>

          <p>
            The other qualifier is how much you value time. A homeowner who enjoys a long, slow mow on a tractor gets little from a zero-turn. One who wants a three-acre lawn done in an hour instead of two will feel the difference every weekend for the life of the machine.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">Rule of thumb</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Over an acre, mostly level, lots of obstacles: zero-turn. Hills, towing or snow: tractor.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A zero-turn is a mowing specialist. If your property asks the machine to do more than mow, the versatility of a tractor usually wins.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="slopes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Slopes, Traction and Safety
          </h2>

          <p>
            Slopes are the single biggest reason a zero-turn turns out to be the wrong purchase. Because they steer only through the rear drive wheels, losing traction on a slope means losing steering and braking at the same time. On wet grass or loose soil the mower can slide sideways or downhill, and the front casters do nothing to stop it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most manufacturers state a maximum recommended slope in the operator manual, commonly in the range of about 10 to 15 degrees for residential zero-turns. Read the number for the specific model you are considering, and be conservative: that figure assumes dry grass, good tires and careful technique. Many owners use a smartphone inclinometer app to check their steepest areas before buying.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most zero-turn manuals direct you to travel straight up and down a slope, not side to side across it, and avoid sharp turns on any incline. Keep the rollover protection structure up and wear the seat belt whenever a ROPS is fitted. Never mow near drop-offs, ditches, retaining walls or pond banks where a wheel could go over an edge.
          </p>

          <p>
            If part of your property is steep, use a walk-behind or a string trimmer on that section. A rear-drive <a href="/best-self-propelled-lawn-mower/">self-propelled mower</a> handles banks a rider should not attempt, and many large-property owners keep one alongside the zero-turn for exactly that purpose. Spending a little more time on the hill is better than putting a heavy machine on ground it was not designed for.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="deck-width" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Choosing a Deck Width That Fits Your Yard and Your Gates
          </h2>

          <p>
            Deck width sets how much grass you cut per pass, but the right size depends on more than acreage. Start by measuring every gate and opening the mower must fit through, plus the garage or shed door where it will live. A 60-inch deck is roughly five and a half feet wide once you include the discharge chute, so it simply will not pass through a standard 48-inch garden gate.
          </p>

          <p>
            Next, think about obstacle density. A wide deck saves time on open lawn but is slower in yards full of trees, beds and narrow strips, because you end up trimming more with the edge of the deck and making extra passes around obstacles. A 48- or 52-inch machine is often faster overall on a busy one-to-two-acre lot than a 60-inch on the same ground.
          </p>

          <p>
            Trailers matter for anyone who moves the mower between properties. Measure the inside width of your trailer between the fenders before choosing a 60-inch deck.
          </p>

          <p>
            As a starting point, 42 to 48 inches suits roughly one acre, 50 to 54 inches suits one to three acres, and 60 inches or more suits three acres and up of open turf. Adjust down for obstacles and gates, and up for long, open runs.
          </p>

