        <div id="storage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            12. Storage, Trailering and the Off-Season
          </h2>

          <p>
            A zero-turn takes more floor space than most buyers expect. Beyond the deck width, measure the overall length including the foot platform and any rear bagger, then check that you can still open a car door or reach the workbench with it parked. Some owners build a simple lean-to or buy a shed specifically for the mower, and the cost of that space belongs in the budget.
          </p>

          <p>
            For trailering, use a ramp rated for the mower plus operator weight, and load the machine by backing up the ramp, which keeps the heavier rear end low and reduces the chance of tipping. Strap the frame, not the deck or the lap bars, and set the parking brake. Many owners strap to the rear frame and the front caster forks at four points.
          </p>

          <p>
            Before winter, run the gas engine dry or fill the tank with fresh fuel treated with stabilizer and run it for a few minutes so treated fuel reaches the carburetor. Change the oil while it is warm, clean the deck, sharpen the blades and charge or remove the battery. A battery maintainer keeps a lead-acid starting battery healthy through cold months.
          </p>

          <p>
            Battery zero-turns need their packs stored indoors in a cool, dry spot at a moderate state of charge, typically around half full, as most manufacturers advise. Do not leave lithium packs in a freezing shed for months. In spring, check tire pressure, look over belts and hoses, and grease any fittings before the first mow.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="price-tiers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            13. What Each Price Tier Buys You
          </h2>

          <p>
            Entry residential zero-turns, roughly $3,000 to $4,500 at the time of writing, typically have stamped decks, entry-level sealed transaxles and basic seats. They are fine for flat lawns of one to two acres mowed weekly by a careful owner. Expect several years of good service if you avoid slopes and heavy use.
          </p>

          <p>
            Upper residential models, roughly $4,500 to $6,500, add fabricated decks, better engines, more comfortable seats and sometimes serviceable transaxles. This tier is where most buyers with two to four acres get the best balance of price and longevity.
          </p>

          <p>
            Prosumer machines, roughly $6,500 to $10,000 or more, borrow from commercial lines: heavy fabricated decks, commercial-grade transaxles, suspension and wider deck options. They make sense for large properties, owners who plan to keep a mower for well over a decade, or anyone who mows several properties.
          </p>

          <p>
            Battery zero-turns span a wide range depending on battery count. Price the configuration you actually need for your acreage, including extra packs, before comparing against gas. And regardless of tier, factor in a trailer, spare blades and dealer service costs when budgeting.
          </p>

