        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            14. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a zero-turn for a hilly property</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Zero-turns steer and brake through the rear wheels only. On slopes and wet grass they can lose traction and slide. Check the manual's maximum slope rating, measure your steepest areas, and use a walk-behind or tractor where the ground is too steep. No mowing deadline is worth a rollover.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing deck width from acreage alone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 60-inch deck sounds efficient for four acres, but it may not fit through your gate, onto your trailer or around tight obstacles. Measure gates, sheds and trailers first, and think about how much of your yard is open turf. Narrower can be faster overall on a busy lot.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the transaxles</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The transaxles decide how long a zero-turn lasts. Entry-level sealed units are fine for light homeowner use but wear quickly under hard work. If you have a lot of acreage or slopes, pay for serviceable or commercial-grade transaxles. Ask the dealer for the transaxle model number and whether it can be serviced.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping blade and deck maintenance</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Dull blades and a dirty, uneven deck cause poor cut quality, scalping and extra engine load. Sharpen blades every 20 to 25 hours, clean the deck after wet mows and check deck level each season to keep the cut clean. Most cut complaints trace back to these basics.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using the zero-turn as a tow vehicle</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most residential zero-turns are not built to tow heavy carts or ground-engaging attachments. Doing so stresses the transaxles and can be unsafe on slopes. If you need towing or attachments, a lawn tractor or compact tractor is the right tool. Light carts on level ground are usually the limit.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            15. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which zero-turn mower should most buyers start with?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most one-to-three-acre properties, the Ariens IKON XD 52 or Husqvarna Z254F give the best mix of fabricated-deck durability and fair pricing. If you mow three acres or more and want a machine that lasts well over a decade, the Toro TITAN MAX is the stronger long-term buy. Dealer proximity should weigh heavily in the final choice.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How wide a deck do I need for two acres?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Around 50 to 54 inches suits most two-acre properties. That width covers ground quickly without being unwieldy around trees and beds. If the property is mostly open with long runs, 60 inches can save time, but check your gates, shed door and trailer width first, because a 60-inch deck will not fit through many standard openings.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What acreage is a residential zero-turn built for?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most residential zero-turns are sized to mow one to five acres comfortably, depending on deck width and build quality. Entry models suit one to two acres; upper residential models handle two to four acres; prosumer machines are designed for larger properties and heavier weekly use. Battery models are generally best on about one to two acres.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use a zero-turn on a hill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only on gentle slopes, and only within the limit stated in the operator manual, which is often about 10 to 15 degrees for residential models. Zero-turns steer through the rear wheels, so they can lose control on wet grass or steep ground. Mow slopes carefully and use a walk-behind or trimmer on steep banks.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a zero-turn mower last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Entry-level residential zero-turns often last several years to roughly a decade with good care. Upper residential and prosumer models with fabricated decks and better transaxles commonly last much longer. Lifespan depends heavily on the transaxles, how much acreage you mow, whether you mow slopes, and how well you keep up with blades, oil and deck cleaning.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is paying more for a fabricated deck worthwhile?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If you mow around trees, roots, rocks or rough edges, usually yes. Fabricated decks are welded from thicker steel and resist dents and rust far better than stamped decks. On a smooth, obstacle-free lawn, a well-kept stamped deck can last a long time, so the upgrade matters less there. Ask the dealer which deck the trim you are quoting actually has.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Zero-turn or lawn tractor: how do I decide?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Choose a zero-turn for mostly level properties over about an acre with lots of trees and beds, where speed and maneuverability save real time. Choose a lawn tractor for hilly ground, towing, snow removal or ground-engaging attachments. Tractors steer with the front wheels and are generally more stable on slopes.
              </p>
            </div>

